
✅ c → create
✅ x → extract
✅ z → gzip compression
✅ j → bzip2 compression
✅ J → xz compression
✅ v → verbose
✅ f → filename
✅ t → list
✅ r → append file(s) to existing tar archive

=================================================================================================================

Q.1> Create a compressed archive file /root/etc_backup.tar.gz of the /etc directory using gzip compression.

A.1> tar -czvf /root/etc_backup.tar.gz /etc

c → create
z → gzip compression
v → verbose
f → filename

===================================================================================================================================================================

Q.2> Extract the above archive /root/etc_backup.tar.gz into /tmp/etc_restore.

A.2> mkdir -p /tmp/etc_restore
     tar -xzvf /root/etc_backup.tar.gz -C /tmp/etc_restore

x → extract
z → gzip decompression
C → target directory

=================================================================================================================================================================

Q.3> Create an archive /root/home_backup.tar.bz2 of the /home directory using bzip2 compression.

A.3> tar -cjvf /root/home_backup.tar.bz2 /home

j → bzip2 compression

================================================================================================================================================================

Q.4> List the contents of the archive /root/home_backup.tar.bz2 without extracting it.

A.4> tar -tjvf /root/home_backup.tar.bz2

t → list

==================================================================================================================================================================

Q.5> You have a file /root/logs.tar.xz. Extract it into /opt/logs.

A.5>  mkdir -p /opt/logs
     tar -xJvf /root/logs.tar.xz -C /opt/logs

J → xz compression

==================================================================================================================================================================

Q.6> Create a tar archive /root/multiple.tar containing the files /etc/hosts, /etc/passwd, and /etc/fstab.

A.6> tar -cvf /root/multiple.tar /etc/hosts /etc/passwd /etc/fstab

===================================================================================================================================================================

Q.7> Add a new file /etc/resolv.conf to the existing archive /root/multiple.tar.

A.7> tar -rvf /root/multiple.tar /etc/resolv.conf

r → append file(s) to existing tar archive

===================================================================================================================================================================

Q.8> What command would you use to compress a single file /var/log/messages using gzip, and then decompress it back?

A.8> 
gzip /var/log/messages      # creates /var/log/messages.gz
gunzip /var/log/messages.gz # restores original file

======================================================================================================================================================================

Q.9> Compress the archive /root/etc_archive.tar using gzip. Name it /root/etc_archive.tar.gz.

A.9> tar -cvf /root/etc_archive.tar /etc/passwd /etc/group /etc/fstab

=========================================================================================================================================================================

Q.10> Extract /root/etc_archive.tar.gz into /tmp/etc_extract.

A.10> mkdir -p /tmp/etc_extract
      tar -xzvf /root/etc_archive.tar.gz -C /tmp/etc_extract

x → extract
z → use gzip
C → extract to target directory
      
========================================================================================================================================================================

Q.11> Create a tar archive /root/home.tar.bz2 of the /home directory using bzip2 compression.

A.11> tar -cjvf /root/home.tar.bz2 /home

=========================================================================================================================================================================

Q.12> Create an archive /root/logs.tar.xz of /var/log using xz compression.

A.12> tar -cJvf /root/logs.tar.xz /var/log

J → use xz compression (best compression ratio)

===========================================================================================================================================================================

Q.13> Add /etc/hosts to the existing uncompressed archive /root/etc_archive.tar.

A.13> tar -rvf /root/etc_archive.tar /etc/hosts

r → append to an existing (uncompressed) tar
⚠️ Note: You cannot append to compressed archives (.tar.gz, .tar.bz2, .tar.xz).

============================================================================================================================================================================

Q.14> Extract /root/logs.tar.xz into /opt/logs_restore.

A.14> mkdir -p /opt/logs_restore
      tar -xJvf /root/logs.tar.xz -C /opt/logs_restore

==========================================================================================================================================================================

Q.15> Create a tar archive /root/multiple_files.tar containing /etc/passwd, /etc/group, /etc/fstab, but exclude files ending with .bak.

A.15> tar -cvf /root/multiple_files.tar /etc/passwd /etc/group /etc/fstab --exclude='*.bak'

--exclude skips files matching pattern (e.g., *.bak)

============================================================================================================================================================================

Q.16> Compress a single file /var/log/messages using gzip, then decompress it back to verify it’s restored.

A.16>

gzip /var/log/messages
gunzip /var/log/messages.gz

gzip compresses single files only
gunzip decompresses them back
